## two-resistors

two-resistors-series-circuit-ask = How do the voltages across the resistors relate to the total?
two-resistors-series-circuit-title = Conductors in series
two-resistors-series-circuit-choices = The total is the sum of the voltages across the resistors
two-resistors-series-circuit-choices-2 = Each resistor gets the source’s whole voltage
two-resistors-series-circuit-choices-3 = It splits in half, whatever the resistors
two-resistors-source-label = Source voltage
two-resistors-source-options = 2 V
two-resistors-source-options-2 = 4 V
two-resistors-source-options-3 = 6 V
two-resistors-build-question = We connect two resistors (2 and 4 Ω) in series — one after the other — with an ammeter and a { $source } V source. We measure the current and the voltage across the first resistor, the second and both together. How do the voltages across the resistors relate to the total?
two-resistors-parallel-circuit-ask = How do the branch currents relate to the total current?
two-resistors-parallel-circuit-title = Conductors in parallel
two-resistors-parallel-circuit-choices = The total is the sum of the branch currents
two-resistors-parallel-circuit-choices-2 = Each branch carries the whole current
two-resistors-parallel-circuit-choices-3 = It splits in half, whatever the resistors
two-resistors-source-options-4 = 1 V
two-resistors-source-options-5 = 1.5 V
two-resistors-source-options-6 = 2.5 V
two-resistors-setup = A source, a key, two resistors (2 and 4 Ω), an ammeter, a voltmeter, connecting wires
two-resistors-parallel-question = We connect the same resistors (2 and 4 Ω) in parallel — each on its own branch — to a { $source } V source. We measure the current in the branch of the first resistor, of the second and before the fork, and the voltage each time. How do the branch currents relate to the total current?
